Dart has a notion of [`IOSink`](http://api.dartlang.org/docs/releases/latest/dart_io/IOSink.html) which basically means anything that is a Writable Stream is actually a object with an `addStream` method

This basically means that `.addStream()` is an implementation of `.pipe()` that lives on the writable stream.

This means 
- you do not have to implement a Writable interface.
- every writable sink (namely every IOSink) can have its own piping algorithm
- every writable sink can talk back pressure directly to the readable source. Namely if FileSource & FileSink have a special protocol they can talk to each other over this internal protocol because `FileSource.addStream(FileSink)` means they both know exactly what's going on with eachother.
- It means there is only one Stream primitive, namely the ReadableStream

This might look like:

``` js
function WritableStream(onWrite, onEnd) {
    this.onWrite = onWrite
    this.onEnd = onEnd
}

WritableStream.prototype.addStream = function (readableStream) {
    // this is .pipe() implementation basically
    var onWrite = this.onWrite
    var onEnd = this.onEnd

    read()

    function read() {
        readableStream.read().then(function (chunk) {
            if (chunk === undefined) return onEnd()

            var backPressure = onWrite(chunk)

            // onWrite may return promise that says only
            // write to me again once this promise resolves
            if (backPressure) {
                return backPressure.then(read)
            }

            read()
        })
    }
}
```

You can still have the duplex piping like in node but in reverse

`res.addStream(gzip().addStream(req))`
